IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT `pea-types`
IMPORT `pea-notice-to-quit`

-- Protection from Eviction Act 1977 (UK) — GOAL 3: UNLAWFUL EVICTION AND
-- HARASSMENT, AND THE FINANCIAL PENALTY (ss 1, 1A, Schedule A1).
--
--   "Is an offence under s 1 made out; may the local housing authority impose
--    a financial penalty, up to how much; and by when must each step be
--    taken?"
--
-- Source: ../registers/source-bundle/ukpga-1977-43.txt — ss 1, 1A, Sch A1.

§ `Goal 3 — the offence and the financial penalty`

§§ `The offence (s 1)`

-- s 1(2) "If any person unlawfully deprives the residential occupier of any
-- premises of his occupation of the premises or any part thereof, or attempts
-- to do so, he shall be guilty of an offence unless he proves that he
-- believed, and had reasonable cause to believe, that the residential
-- occupier had ceased to reside in the premises."
@ref s 1(1)-(2)
GIVEN c IS A `Conduct`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `unlawful eviction under s 1(2) is made out by` c IF
        c's `the person is a residential occupier`
    AND c's `unlawfully deprived, or attempted to deprive, the occupier of occupation`
    AND NOT (c's `proves a reasonable belief the occupier had ceased to reside`)

-- s 1(3): any person who, with intent to cause the occupier to give up
-- occupation or refrain from a right or remedy, "does acts likely to
-- interfere with the peace or comfort of the residential occupier or members
-- of his household, or persistently withdraws or withholds services".
@ref s 1(3)
GIVEN c IS A `Conduct`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `harassment under s 1(3) is made out by` c IF
        c's `the person is a residential occupier`
    AND c's `intended the occupier to give up occupation or refrain from a right`
    AND (   c's `did acts likely to interfere with peace or comfort`
         OR c's `persistently withdrew or withheld services`)

-- s 1(3A): the landlord or an agent who does such acts, knowing or having
-- reasonable cause to believe the conduct likely to have that effect; (3B):
-- not guilty if they prove reasonable grounds.
@ref s 1(3A)-(3C)
GIVEN c IS A `Conduct`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `harassment by a landlord under s 1(3A) is made out by` c IF
        c's `the person is a residential occupier`
    AND c's `is the landlord or the landlord's agent`
    AND (   c's `did acts likely to interfere with peace or comfort`
         OR c's `persistently withdrew or withheld services`)
    AND c's `knew or had reasonable cause to believe it likely to have that effect`
    AND NOT (c's `proves reasonable grounds for the conduct`)

@ref s 1(2), (3), (3A)
@export Whether conduct amounts to an offence under s 1 of the Protection from Eviction Act 1977 (UK): unlawful eviction of a residential occupier (s 1(2), unless a reasonable belief they had left is proved), harassment with intent (s 1(3)), or harassment by the landlord or an agent who knew or had reasonable cause to believe it likely to drive the occupier out (s 1(3A), unless reasonable grounds are proved). DRAFT, not reviewed by a UK lawyer.
GIVEN c IS A `Conduct`  @desc What was done, with what intent or knowledge, and any defence proved
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `an offence under s 1 is made out by` c IF
        `unlawful eviction under s 1(2) is made out by` c
    OR  `harassment under s 1(3) is made out by` c
    OR  `harassment by a landlord under s 1(3A) is made out by` c

§§ `The financial penalty (s 1A)`

-- s 1A(1) "A local housing authority may impose a financial penalty on a
-- person if satisfied beyond reasonable doubt that the person has committed
-- an offence under section 1 in relation to premises in England." (2): not
-- where the person has been convicted, proceedings are pending, or they
-- ended without conviction.
@ref s 1A(1)-(2)
@export Whether a local housing authority may impose a financial penalty under s 1A of the Protection from Eviction Act 1977 (UK): an offence under s 1 in relation to premises in England, of which the authority is satisfied beyond reasonable doubt, and no conviction, pending proceedings or proceedings concluded without conviction for the conduct. DRAFT, not reviewed by a UK lawyer.
GIVEN c IS A `Conduct`        @desc The conduct
      p IS A `Penalty facts`  @desc England, the authority's satisfaction, and the state of any criminal proceedings
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `a financial penalty may be imposed for` c `with` p IF
        `an offence under s 1 is made out by` c
    AND p's `premises in England`
    AND p's `authority satisfied beyond reasonable doubt`
    AND NOT (p's `convicted for the conduct`)
    AND NOT (p's `criminal proceedings pending`)
    AND NOT (p's `criminal proceedings concluded without conviction`)

-- s 1A(3) "The amount of a financial penalty ... must not be more than
-- £40,000."
@ref s 1A(3)
`the maximum financial penalty` MEANS 40000

§§ `The steps in Schedule A1`

-- "The period of 6 months beginning with" day x: it ends on the day before
-- the corresponding date six months later, or, if that month has none, on
-- its last day.
GIVEN x IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`the last day of the 6 months beginning with` x MEANS
    IF   DATE_DAY c EQUALS DATE_DAY x
    THEN `the day before` c
    ELSE c
    WHERE
        c MEANS `add months` x 6

-- Sch A1 para 1 "(1) The notice of intent must be given before the end of the
-- period of 6 months beginning with the first day on which the authority has
-- sufficient evidence of the conduct to which the financial penalty relates.
-- (2) But if the person is continuing to engage in the conduct on that day,
-- and the conduct continues beyond the end of that day, the notice of intent
-- may be given— (a) at any time when the conduct is continuing, or (b) within
-- the period of 6 months beginning with the last day on which the conduct
-- occurs." NOTHING: the conduct is still continuing, so there is no last day
-- yet.
@ref Sch A1 para 1
@export The last day on which a local housing authority may give a notice of intent under Schedule A1 para 1 to the Protection from Eviction Act 1977 (UK): the last day of the six months beginning with the first day it had sufficient evidence; for conduct continuing beyond that day, the last day of the six months beginning with the last day of the conduct (NOTHING while it continues, when notice may be given at any time). DRAFT, not reviewed by a UK lawyer.
GIVEN p IS A `Penalty facts`  @desc The first day of sufficient evidence, whether the conduct continued beyond it, and the last day of the conduct
GIVETH A MAYBE DATE
`the last day for the notice of intent for` p MEANS
    IF   p's `conduct continuing on that day and beyond it`
    THEN CONSIDER p's `last day of the conduct`
         WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING
         WHEN JUST last THEN JUST (`the later of` firstLimit (`the last day of the 6 months beginning with` last))
    ELSE JUST firstLimit
    WHERE
        firstLimit MEANS `the last day of the 6 months beginning with` (p's `first day of sufficient evidence`)

-- Sch A1 para 4(2) "Any representations must be made within the period of 28
-- days beginning with the day after that on which the notice was given";
-- para 5: the final notice comes after the end of that period, and "must
-- require the penalty to be paid within the period of 28 days beginning with
-- the day after that on which the notice was given"; para 10(2): an appeal
-- "must be brought within the period of 28 days beginning with the day after
-- that on which the final notice was given". Each period's last day is the
-- day of giving + 28.
@ref Sch A1 paras 4(2), 5, 10(2)
GIVEN d IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`the last of the 28 days beginning with the day after` d MEANS
    `the day` 28 `days after` d

DECLARE `Penalty dates` HAS
    `last day for the notice of intent`                 IS A MAYBE DATE
    `last day for representations`                      IS A MAYBE DATE
    `earliest day for the final notice`                 IS A MAYBE DATE
    `last day for payment`                              IS A MAYBE DATE
    `last day to appeal`                                IS A MAYBE DATE

GIVEN n IS A NUMBER, d IS A MAYBE DATE
GIVETH A MAYBE DATE
`the day` n `days after, if given,` d MEANS
    CONSIDER d
    WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING
    WHEN JUST x  THEN JUST (`the day` n `days after` x)

@ref Sch A1 paras 1, 4, 5, 10
@export The dates in Schedule A1 to the Protection from Eviction Act 1977 (UK) for a financial penalty under s 1A: the last day for the notice of intent (para 1); the last day for representations, 28 days after the notice of intent (para 4(2)); the earliest day for the final notice, after that period ends (para 5); the last day for payment and the last day to appeal, each 28 days after the final notice (paras 5, 10(2)). Each is NOTHING where the notice it runs from has not been given. DRAFT, not reviewed by a UK lawyer.
GIVEN p IS A `Penalty facts`  @desc The evidence and conduct dates, and the days the notice of intent and final notice were given
GIVETH A `Penalty dates`
`the penalty dates for` p MEANS
    `Penalty dates` WITH
        `last day for the notice of intent`             IS `the last day for the notice of intent for` p
        `last day for representations`                  IS `the day` 28 `days after, if given,` (p's `notice of intent given on`)
        `earliest day for the final notice`             IS `the day` 29 `days after, if given,` (p's `notice of intent given on`)
        `last day for payment`                          IS `the day` 28 `days after, if given,` (p's `final notice given on`)
        `last day to appeal`                            IS `the day` 28 `days after, if given,` (p's `final notice given on`)
